.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-disputes-reasons - List the dispute reasons: the codes --reason accepts


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 disputes reasons [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
List the dispute-reason dictionary: the codes 'rail0 payments dispute' and
\&'rail0 payments dispute close' accept for --reason, with their descriptions.

.PP
There are two lists. A dispute is OPENED with an 'open' reason and CLOSED with a
\&'close' reason; 'other' is in both. The 'system' reason (full_refund) is never
chosen: the protocol records it when a full refund closes an open dispute, and it
is listed so a dispute's close_reason_code can be read.

.PP
On-chain each reason is the bytes32 keccak256("rail0.dispute."); --json (or
piped output) adds it to every row. The list is the one this CLI was built with —
the gateway's is authoritative.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for reasons

.PP
\fB--kind\fP=""
	Only one list: open, close or system


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 disputes reasons
  rail0 disputes reasons --kind close
  rail0 disputes reasons --json
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-disputes(1)\fP
